title: Sync open questions
tags: Sync
kind: question
status: deferred
summary: The batch-size defaults, the matching of records without a position, and the content changes a stamp cannot see.

!! Batch sizes

`commit-batch-size` (50) and `transfer-batch-size` (20) were never measured
([[How a run is batched]]). A benchmark would trade the cost of a commit — a disk
sync — against the work a crash makes the next run redo.

!! Records without a position

Matching a record with no `tree_ref` field asks for an exact twin among the other
side's unlinked, positionless records ([[Matching records across repositories]]).
It is deliberately minimal: any field edited on one side before the first sync
defeats it, and the record is duplicated. A tolerant match — a similarity of fields —
would want a threshold, which the intents file does not have.

!! Content a stamp cannot see

A side's content counts as changed when its size, mtime, kind or link target did
([[Change detection in sync]]). A rewrite of the same size within the same second is
missed, as by the watcher. A content hash in the stamp would see it, at the cost of
hashing every changed file on both sides — which the planner, comparing bytes only
when a stamp moved, avoids today.

!! A file moved before its first sync

A file moved on one side before it was ever linked is matched by its new path, and
its old position on the other side is not recognised as the same bytes: it is synced
as a new file, and the old one stays. Matching by content was ruled out for
everything else ([[Matching records across repositories]]); for this one case it
would need a hash of every unmatched file.
